From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: bonsai-27b-fusion <noreply@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 1 Jan 2026 00:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] feat(fusion): complete the TurboQuant fusion, the cache types
 were unusable

c6fbb78 ported the TurboQuant types and kernels but not the integration, so
`--cache-type-k turbo3` was rejected by the CLI and none of it could be reached:

    error while handling argument "-ctk": Unsupported cache type: turbo3
    allowed values: f32, f16, bf16, q8_0, q4_0, q4_1, iq4_nl, q5_0, q5_1

This adds the missing layer. Taken from the verified fusion/turboquant-on-b10964
branch rather than from the TurboQuant fork, which has moved on since.

* common/arg.cpp: register turbo3/turbo4 in kv_cache_types. Two lines, and the
  ones that actually unblock everything.
* ggml/src/ggml-cuda/fattn.cu: dispatch the 8 turbo K/V pairs in both branches of
  the FA_ALL_QUANTS switch, and accept the types in ggml_cuda_fattn_kv_type_supported.
  The instances are compiled unconditionally (explicit list in the CMakeLists), so
  the non-FA_ALL_QUANTS branch needs them too.
* ggml/src/ggml-cuda/convert.cu: 2 cases in each of the 6 to_fp16/fp32/bf16 tables.
* ggml/src/ggml-cuda/set-rows.cu: the turbo KV write kernel.
* src/llama-graph.cpp: the rotations. WHT on q before every build_attn_mha call
  (all 7 overloads, not just the 5 the older branch has), the inverse WHT on the
  FA output and on kqv in the non-FA path.
* ggml/src/ggml-cpu/{ggml-cpu.c,ops.cpp,ops.h}: CPU traits, vec_dot, and the
  SET_ROWS WHT group-size handoff.
* ggml/src/ggml.c: the two ggml_quantize_chunk cases. The switch is
  `default: assert(false)`, so without them runtime quantization aborts.
* ggml/include/ggml.h: declare ggml_turbo_wht. It was defined in ggml.c but never
  declared, so llama-graph could not have called it.
* ggml/{src,src/ggml-cuda,src/ggml-hip}/CMakeLists.txt: also removed a duplicated
  FA-instance block. The port had appended its own copy, leaving two
  `if (GGML_CUDA_FA_ALL_QUANTS)` blocks back to back; the second one omitted the
  turbo instances.
* tools/llama-bench/llama-bench.cpp: turbo3/turbo4 name mapping, without which the
  performance gate cannot run.
* tests/: test-turbo-wht.cpp and test-turbo-quant-cpu.cpp plus the CMake wiring for
  `ctest -R test-turbo`. Note the HIP build is configured with
  LLAMA_BUILD_TESTS=OFF.

Verification (RX 6900 XT, gfx1030, ROCm 7.1):
  BUILD_OK
  llama-server --help    -> turbo3, turbo4 accepted as cache types
  llama-bench -p 256 -n 32 -r 2 on qwen2.5-1.5B q8_0:
      f16 7801.5/174.1   q8_0 7635.8/160.7   turbo3 7449.1/156.5   turbo4 7467.7/151.0
  perplexity, -c 512 --chunks 8 -ngl 999 -fa 1, qwen2.5-1.5B:
      f16    10.6801   correct
      q8_0   329.4764  broken
      turbo3 8038.7921  broken

Those last two are NOT caused by this port. The same q8_0 KV command on the
original PrismML build, from which this worktree was forked, returns 329.4764 with
byte-identical per-chunk values, so quantized KV is already broken on this base.
And production models are unaffected: SC117 (head_dim 256) generates identical text
with q8_0, turbo3 and turbo4 KV on the promoted b10335 runtime. The 1.5B fixture is
the only model here with head_dim 128, which is where quantized KV diverges.
